A||||13||fierowner||Gears scraping||||8||
Z||000000||fierowner||03-14-2000||07:40 PM||||I have an 84 SE 4 spd. and here's my story: The gears shift easily when the car is turned off. We turn it on, Reverse scrapes, 1st scrapes, and 3rd and 4th are so tight that it is almost impossible to get the stick over to them. This is a big problem for me, and I need to know what i can do to fix it ASAP.  I have the haynes repair manual and use it quite frequently...the illustrations in this sections are horrible.  Any help appreciated.||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||03-14-2000||08:06 PM||||Sounds like the clutch isn't fully releasing. Biggest possibilities are bent clutch pedal, quite common with the aluminum ones(were in the '84s) or you have air in the line. Try bleeding the line, and check for a bent pedal, are my suggestions.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||03-14-2000||08:17 PM||||Gotta be the pedal. I replaced the one in my 86SE and it shifted 111.362% better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||ROB MAC||03-14-2000||08:20 PM||||go to v8 archie web site and find the archism that deals with clutch pedal replacement, print it and DO it. your car will shift like new. place a magnet on your clutch pedal shaft...if it doesnt stick you need to replace it!||||reg||1
Z||000004||kong||03-14-2000||09:11 PM||||could be the pressure plate too. Mine did the same thing when I first got it. I hope that's not it, but it is a possibilty. good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||03-14-2000||09:51 PM||||Another common clutch problem with 84 and 85 Fieros is the clutch slave bracket cracking. This is mounted on the tranny. With the engine off, have someone push in the clutch while you watch the slave unit for movement. Those brackets were weak and flexed a lot until they crack. Solution if this is the problem: order one of Rodney Dickman's steel reinforced slave brackets.||||reg||1
Z||000006||William||03-15-2000||12:53 AM||||I had your problem but the solution is one that I never see posted for this problem. Look at your clutch pedal. It should be about 3/4 of an inch higher than the brake pedal. If it is level than check if you still have the original aluminum pedal and see if it is bent at the top. This was not my problem as mine seemed to have already been replaced with the steel one. My pedal was too low because the master cylinder had internal corrsion causing the piston not to return back and fill the bore completely. This gave me less throw and would not fully disengage the clutch. A new master cured the problem.||||reg||4
Z||000007||ROB MAC||03-15-2000||01:10 PM||||I think youll find the pedal replacement is your problem but one more note..mine was left for so long that when I finally did replace it the slave cylinder was so cruddy inside from not fully extending that it blew out the seals .It had to be replaced $$$||||reg||1
Z||000008||DXR_DAD||03-15-2000||08:17 PM||||I don't say much, but I do read alot of posts just to see if it's something that I may need, THIS IS ONE that I have seen many times. Replace pedal, replace pedal, replace pedal....OK I do not have a clutch prob.....yet?? but I would like to replace my clutch pedal with the steel one before I do have a problem. This pedal thing has been on my mind for some time now so if anyone has one they would like to sell E-mail me.<P>THANKS!||||reg||1
Z||000009||ROB MAC||03-17-2000||08:12 PM||||I bought mine from GM parts counter . V8 archie website (archism 8 I think ) gives all details including part numbers||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||03-19-2000||12:23 AM||||I know, replace everything and that will cure your problem. I work’s for everyone else, right.||||reg||1
